/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/server-plus.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("server-plus", [["sv1",0,1,"An admin is filling an empty 42U rack with a UPS, a storage array and several 1U servers. Where should the UPS go?",["At the top, near the ceiling for cooling","In the middle, at eye level for easy reading","In a separate rack beside the servers","At the bottom of the rack"],3,"Heavy items such as UPS units go at the bottom to keep the rack's center of gravity low. Mounting them high makes the rack top-heavy and risky when servers slide out.","Objective 1.1",["Mounting a heavy UPS high makes the rack top-heavy and prone to tipping; cooling is not improved by height.","Eye-level placement is a convenience, not a safety rule, and it still raises the rack's center of gravity.","A UPS belongs at the bottom of the same rack; putting it in a separate rack wastes space and is unnecessary.",null],null],["sv2",0,1,"A data center uses a hot aisle/cold aisle layout. Which way should the servers be installed?",["Fronts facing the hot aisle so exhaust is cooled","Fronts facing the cold aisle so intake draws cool air","Alternating directions in each rack","Sideways so air flows freely between neighbouring racks and rows"],1,"Servers draw cool air in through the front and exhaust from the back, so fronts face the cold aisle. Facing the hot aisle would pull in exhaust air.","Objective 1.1",["Facing fronts into the hot aisle makes intakes pull in warm exhaust air, causing the servers to overheat.",null,"Alternating rack directions mixes hot exhaust with cold intake, defeating the hot/cold aisle separation.","Mounting servers sideways breaks the aisle airflow design and lets exhaust recirculate into intakes."],null],["sv3",0,1,"A server has two power supplies. To remove single points of failure in the power path, how should they be connected?",["Each into a separate PDU fed by a separate circuit","Both into the same PDU on one UPS","One into a PDU and one into a wall outlet","Both into a surge-protected power strip plugged into a single UPS"],0,"Separate PDUs on separate circuits (A and B feeds) mean one PDU, UPS or breaker failure does not take the server down.","Objective 1.1",[null,"One PDU on a single UPS is itself a single point of failure, so a fault there still drops the server.","A wall outlet is unconditioned and unprotected, and mixing it with a PDU leaves an unbalanced power path.","A power strip and a single UPS are both single points of failure feeding both supplies at once."],null],["sv4",0,1,"Six 4 TB drives are configured as RAID 6. What is the usable capacity?",["24 TB","20 TB","16 TB","12 TB"],2,"RAID 6 uses two drives' worth of capacity for dual parity: (6 − 2) × 4 TB = 16 TB. RAID 5 would give 20 TB.","Objective 1.2",["24 TB is the raw total (or RAID 0) and ignores the capacity that dual parity consumes.","20 TB is the RAID 5 result, which reserves only one drive of parity, not the two RAID 6 uses.",null,"12 TB subtracts three drives' worth of capacity; RAID 6 only reserves two for its dual parity."],null],["sv5",0,1,"A database server needs the best write performance and must survive a drive failure. Rebuild time should be short. Which RAID level fits?",["RAID 10","RAID 5","RAID 0","RAID 6 with a hot spare"],0,"RAID 10 stripes across mirrors, so writes have no parity penalty and rebuilds just copy a mirror. RAID 5 and 6 add parity write penalties and long rebuilds.","Objective 1.2",[null,"RAID 5 adds a parity write penalty and rebuilds slowly by recalculating parity across all drives.","RAID 0 has no redundancy at all, so a single drive failure destroys the entire array.","RAID 6's dual parity slows writes further and its rebuilds are long, even with a hot spare on hand."],null],["sv6",0,1,"Which storage option presents block-level LUNs to servers over a standard Ethernet network?",["NAS using SMB","iSCSI SAN","DAS over SAS","NFS export"],1,"iSCSI carries SCSI block commands over TCP/IP, so servers see LUNs as local disks. NAS protocols such as SMB and NFS share files, not blocks.","Objective 1.2",["NAS with SMB shares data at the file level over the network, not as raw block-level LUNs.",null,"DAS over SAS is block storage but is attached directly to one server, not delivered over Ethernet.","NFS is a file-level network share, so servers see mounted folders rather than block LUNs."],null],["sv7",0,1,"An admin must replace a failed drive in a production server without shutting it down. Which feature must the drive bay and controller support?",["Write-back cache","Secure Boot","Hot-swap","Wake-on-LAN"],2,"Hot-swappable bays let a drive be removed and replaced while the system runs. Write-back cache improves performance but does not allow live replacement.","Objective 1.2",["Write-back cache boosts write performance but does nothing to allow replacing a drive while powered on.","Secure Boot verifies signed boot components and is unrelated to live drive replacement.",null,"Wake-on-LAN powers a system on remotely and has nothing to do with hot-swapping a drive."],null],["sv8",0,1,"A new 8 TB drive shows as about 7.28 TB in the operating system. What explains the difference?",["Space reserved for the RAID controller","A failing drive with bad sectors","The file system journal","Base-10 vs base-2 capacity calculation"],3,"Vendors count 1 TB as 10^12 bytes; the OS reports in 2^40-byte units. 8 × 10^12 / 2^40 ≈ 7.28.","Objective 1.2",["No RAID is mentioned, and controllers do not reserve enough space to explain the shortfall.","The reduced figure is normal reporting math, not a symptom of a failing drive with bad sectors.","A file system journal is tiny and cannot account for hundreds of gigabytes of apparent difference.",null],null],["sv9",0,1,"A remote server has hung and the OS is unreachable over the network. Which tool lets the admin power-cycle it and watch POST remotely?",["RDP","The BMC, such as iDRAC or iLO","SSH","SNMP traps sent to the monitoring console"],1,"A baseboard management controller runs independently of the OS on its own network port, providing remote power control and console. RDP and SSH need a working OS.","Objective 1.3",["RDP relies on a running OS and network stack, so it cannot help when the OS is unreachable.",null,"SSH also needs a working OS and service, which is exactly what has failed here.","SNMP traps only push alert messages; they cannot power-cycle hardware or display the POST screen."],null],["sv10",0,1,"Which setting should be enabled in UEFI to ensure only signed bootloaders and OS kernels load?",["Secure Boot","Legacy boot","PXE boot","Fast boot"],0,"Secure Boot checks signatures on boot components against trusted keys. Legacy (CSM) boot disables that check.","Objective 1.3",[null,"Legacy (CSM) boot disables signature checking, which is the opposite of what is wanted.","PXE boot loads an OS or installer over the network and does not enforce signed bootloaders.","Fast boot skips parts of startup for speed and does not validate boot component signatures."],null],["sv11",0,1,"An admin needs to connect two top-of-rack switches with a short, low-cost 10 Gbps link. Which option fits best?",["Single-mode fiber with LR optics","A long Cat5e patch cable run through the overhead tray","Twinax direct attach copper (DAC) cable","Coaxial RG-6 cable"],2,"A passive DAC is inexpensive and ideal for short 10 Gbps runs between SFP+ ports. LR optics work but cost far more for a few meters.","Objective 1.1",["Single-mode fiber with LR optics works but costs far more than needed for a few-meter link.","Cat5e is not rated for reliable 10 Gbps and is a poor fit for a short, high-speed switch interconnect.",null,"RG-6 coax is for video/CATV signaling and cannot carry a 10 Gbps SFP+ switch link."],null],["sv12",0,1,"Before updating a server's firmware, what should the admin do first?",["Disable the host firewall","Reset the BIOS to defaults","Remove the redundant power supplies to avoid a surge during flashing","Check release notes and compatibility, and have a rollback plan"],3,"Firmware updates can break drivers or hardware support, so review the notes, test and plan rollback in a maintenance window. Resetting BIOS defaults may undo needed settings.","Objective 1.3",["Disabling the host firewall has no bearing on flashing firmware and only weakens security.","Resetting the BIOS to defaults would wipe needed settings rather than prepare for the update.","Removing power supplies risks losing power mid-flash, which can brick the firmware.",null],null],["sv13",0,1,"A server needs memory that can detect and correct single-bit errors. What should be ordered?",["Non-parity desktop DIMMs","ECC memory","Faster-clocked unbuffered DIMMs","Extra swap space on SSD"],1,"Error-correcting code memory detects and fixes single-bit errors, which is standard for servers. Faster non-ECC memory offers no correction.","Objective 1.3",["Non-parity desktop DIMMs cannot detect or correct any memory errors.",null,"Faster unbuffered non-ECC memory offers speed but no error correction, which servers need.","Adding SSD swap is disk paging and does nothing to correct in-memory bit errors."],null],["sv14",0,1,"A small office has no rack space and needs one quiet, standalone server under a desk. Which form factor fits?",["Blade","2U rack mount","Tower","1U rack mount"],2,"Tower servers stand alone and are quieter, suiting offices without racks. Blades need an enclosure and rack servers need rails and a rack.","Objective 1.1",["A blade server requires an enclosure and a rack, which this office does not have.","A 2U rack-mount server still needs a rack and rail kit to install.",null,"A 1U rack server needs a rack and its small high-speed fans are loud for an office."],null],["sv15",0,1,"An admin needs to know how long a UPS will run the rack during an outage. Which two values matter most?",["Battery capacity and the total load in watts","Rack height and number of servers","Input voltage, outlet type and the rack's physical height","Server CPU speed and RAM"],0,"Runtime depends on how much energy the batteries hold and how much power the load draws. Outlet types matter for connection, not runtime.","Objective 1.1",[null,"Rack height and server count describe physical size, not how long the batteries can sustain the load.","Voltage and outlet type matter for connection, and physical height is irrelevant to runtime energy math.","CPU speed and RAM do not directly give the wattage draw needed to calculate runtime."],null],["sv16",0,1,"Which drive interface offers the lowest latency by connecting flash storage directly over PCIe lanes?",["SATA","SAS","USB 3","NVMe"],3,"NVMe was designed for flash and uses PCIe directly, avoiding the SATA/SAS controller layers. SAS is enterprise-grade but still slower than NVMe.","Objective 1.2",["SATA is an older AHCI interface with higher latency than a direct PCIe flash connection.","SAS is enterprise-grade but still routes through a controller layer, making it slower than NVMe.","USB 3 is an external peripheral bus, not a low-latency internal storage path.",null],null],["sv17",0,2,"An admin must install the same OS image on 40 new servers over the network without media. Which method fits?",["Install from USB on each one","PXE boot to a deployment server","Clone with a Type 2 hypervisor","Use a P2V conversion from a reference server"],1,"PXE lets servers boot from the network and pull an image or unattended install from a deployment server. USB installs do not scale to 40 servers.","Objective 2.1",["Installing from USB on each of 40 servers is manual and does not scale.",null,"A Type 2 hypervisor runs on a desktop OS and is not a tool for mass bare-metal deployment.","P2V converts an existing physical server to a VM; it does not deploy an OS to 40 new machines."],null],["sv18",0,2,"Before buying new servers for an OS, what should the admin check to confirm the hardware is supported?",["The SLA","The OS end-user license agreement terms","The hardware compatibility list","The asset inventory"],2,"The HCL lists hardware the OS vendor has tested and supports. The license covers usage rights, not hardware support.","Objective 2.1",["An SLA defines service commitments and says nothing about which hardware the OS supports.","The EULA covers usage rights, not whether the hardware has been tested by the OS vendor.",null,"The asset inventory lists owned equipment, not vendor-tested compatibility for a new OS."],null],["sv19",0,2,"A company wants to reduce the attack surface and resource use of Windows file servers managed remotely. Which installation option fits?",["Server Core","Desktop Experience","A Type 2 hypervisor","A 32-bit edition"],0,"Server Core has no full GUI, so it uses fewer resources and has fewer components to patch. Desktop Experience adds the GUI and its attack surface.","Objective 2.1",[null,"Desktop Experience adds the full GUI, increasing both resource use and attack surface.","A Type 2 hypervisor is a virtualization product, not a Windows Server installation option.","A 32-bit edition limits memory and is not how you reduce attack surface; Server is 64-bit anyway."],null],["sv20",0,2,"A physical legacy server must be moved onto a hypervisor with minimal rebuild work. What process is used?",["V2P","PXE install","Bare-metal restore","P2V"],3,"Physical-to-virtual conversion copies the running server's disks and configuration into a VM. V2P is the reverse direction.","Objective 2.1",["V2P is the reverse process, converting a virtual machine back to physical hardware.","A PXE install builds a fresh OS over the network, which would mean rebuilding, not migrating.","A bare-metal restore recovers a backup onto hardware; it does not convert the server into a VM.",null],null],["sv21",0,2,"A Linux file server needs a file system that handles very large files and volumes well and is the default on RHEL. Which one fits?",["FAT32","exFAT","XFS","NTFS"],2,"XFS is RHEL's default file system and scales to large files and volumes. FAT32 limits files to 4 GB.","Objective 2.1",["FAT32 limits single files to 4 GB and is unsuitable for large server volumes.","exFAT targets removable flash media, not large enterprise Linux file servers.",null,"NTFS is the Windows file system and is not the default on RHEL."],null],["sv22",0,2,"A server's two NICs are combined so traffic continues if one link or switch port fails. What is this called?",["VLAN tagging","NIC teaming","Port mirroring","Jumbo frames"],1,"NIC teaming (bonding) groups adapters for failover and often load balancing. Port mirroring copies traffic for analysis.","Objective 2.2",["VLAN tagging segments traffic into virtual LANs; it does not provide link failover.",null,"Port mirroring copies traffic to a monitoring port and offers no redundancy.","Jumbo frames raise the MTU for throughput and do nothing for link failover."],null],["sv23",0,2,"A new domain controller keeps reporting Kerberos authentication failures, and its clock is 12 minutes off. Which service should be configured?",["DHCP","SNMP","SMTP","NTP"],3,"Kerberos tolerates only small clock skew (5 minutes by default), so servers must sync time with NTP. SNMP is for monitoring.","Objective 2.2",["DHCP assigns IP addresses and has no role in synchronizing the server's clock.","SNMP is for monitoring devices, not for keeping time in sync for Kerberos.","SMTP transports email and is unrelated to clock skew or authentication.",null],null],["sv24",0,2,"A printer server needs the same IP address every time, but the admin wants to manage it from the DHCP server. What should be created?",["A DHCP reservation","A DHCP exclusion","A new scope","A DNS CNAME record"],0,"A reservation ties an IP address to the device's MAC so it always gets the same lease. An exclusion just removes addresses from the pool.","Objective 2.2",[null,"An exclusion removes addresses from the pool but does not assign a fixed address to a device.","A new scope defines a range of addresses, not a guaranteed fixed lease for one device.","A CNAME is a DNS alias and does not control which IP address the printer receives."],null],["sv25",0,2,"A hypervisor host has one physical NIC connected to a trunk port. VMs on it must sit in different VLANs. What must be configured?",["VLAN IDs on the virtual switch port groups or VM adapters","A separate physical switch and uplink cable for every VM","NIC teaming on each guest","A DHCP relay on the host"],0,"Tagging traffic with VLAN IDs on the virtual switch lets one trunked uplink carry several VLANs. Separate switches per VM are unnecessary.","Objective 2.2",[null,"A separate physical switch per VM is unnecessary because a trunked uplink already carries all VLANs.","NIC teaming on the guest aggregates links for redundancy, not VLAN separation.","A DHCP relay forwards DHCP requests across subnets; it does not place VMs into VLANs."],null],["sv26",0,2,"Two web servers sit behind a load balancer. One often has many long-running sessions while the other is idle. Which algorithm balances this better than round robin?",["Source IP hash","Least connections","Static weighting by server age","DNS round robin"],1,"Least connections sends new requests to the server with the fewest active sessions, adapting to long-lived connections. Round robin ignores current load.","Objective 2.4",["Source IP hash pins each client to a server by address and does not even out uneven session lengths.",null,"Weighting by server age is arbitrary and ignores how busy each server actually is.","DNS round robin just rotates returned records with no awareness of current server load."],null],["sv27",0,2,"A two-node cluster lost its network link between nodes, and both tried to own the shared disk. What prevents this?",["Round-robin DNS","A heartbeat alone","A quorum with a witness","NIC teaming on the storage network"],2,"A witness gives one side the majority vote, so only one partition keeps running and split-brain is avoided. A heartbeat detects failure but cannot break the tie.","Objective 2.4",["Round-robin DNS distributes name lookups and does nothing to prevent split-brain.","A heartbeat alone can detect a node failure but cannot break the tie over shared-disk ownership.",null,"NIC teaming on the storage network adds link redundancy but does not arbitrate cluster ownership."],null],["sv28",0,2,"A standby node sits idle until the primary database server fails, then takes over. What configuration is this?",["Active-active","Scale-out clustering","Load balanced","Active-passive"],3,"In active-passive clustering the passive node waits to take over on failure. Active-active runs all nodes at once.","Objective 2.4",["In active-active clustering all nodes serve traffic at once, not one sitting idle.","Scale-out clustering adds nodes to grow capacity, not a passive standby waiting to take over.","Load balancing spreads work across active nodes rather than keeping one on standby.",null],null],["sv29",0,2,"A server connects to a SAN over two HBAs and two fabrics. What software provides failover and load sharing across both paths?",["RAID","NIC teaming","MPIO","iSCSI CHAP"],2,"Multipath I/O manages several paths to the same LUN, failing over and balancing between them. CHAP only authenticates iSCSI sessions.","Objective 2.4",["RAID provides disk redundancy within an array, not failover across SAN fabric paths.","NIC teaming aggregates Ethernet adapters and does not manage storage HBA paths.",null,"iSCSI CHAP only authenticates iSCSI sessions and provides no path failover or load sharing."],null],["sv30",0,2,"A company wants to run production VMs directly on server hardware without a host OS. Which hypervisor type fits?",["Emulator","Type 2","Container runtime","Type 1"],3,"Type 1 (bare-metal) hypervisors such as ESXi, Hyper-V and KVM run directly on hardware. Type 2 runs on top of a desktop OS.","Objective 2.5",["An emulator simulates hardware and is slow, not a bare-metal production hypervisor.","A Type 2 hypervisor runs on top of a host desktop OS, not directly on the hardware.","A container runtime shares the host kernel and is not a hardware-level hypervisor.",null],null],["sv31",0,2,"A host has 64 GB of RAM, but its VMs are assigned 96 GB in total. What is this practice called?",["Memory ballooning failure","Memory overcommitment","Thin provisioning","NUMA pinning"],1,"Assigning more virtual resources than physically exist is overcommitment; it works while VMs do not use their full allocation at once. Thin provisioning refers to storage.","Objective 2.5",["Ballooning is a technique to reclaim guest memory, not the term for assigning more than exists.",null,"Thin provisioning refers to allocating storage on demand, not to RAM allocation.","NUMA pinning binds a VM to specific CPU/memory nodes and is unrelated to overallocation."],null],["sv32",0,2,"An admin wants a quick rollback point before patching a VM. What should they take?",["A snapshot","A clone to a new host","A full backup to tape","A template"],0,"A snapshot captures the VM's state for a fast revert and should be deleted after the change. Templates are for deploying new VMs.","Objective 2.5",[null,"Cloning makes a full copy to another host, which is slower than a snapshot for a quick rollback.","A full backup to tape is slow and meant for disaster recovery, not a fast pre-patch revert.","A template is a master image for deploying new VMs, not a rollback point for this one."],null],["sv33",0,2,"A VM's virtual disk is configured to use physical space only as data is written. What is this?",["Thick provisioning","Thin provisioning","Deduplication","Pass-through disk"],1,"Thin-provisioned disks grow on demand, saving space but risking the datastore filling up. Thick provisioning allocates everything at creation.","Objective 2.5",["Thick provisioning allocates all the disk's space up front, the opposite of growing on demand.",null,"Deduplication removes duplicate data blocks and is a different storage-efficiency feature.","A pass-through disk maps a physical disk directly to the VM and is not about on-demand growth."],null],["sv34",0,2,"A company rents VMs from a cloud provider and manages the OS, patches and apps itself. Which service model is this?",["SaaS","PaaS","FaaS","IaaS"],3,"In IaaS the provider runs hardware and virtualization while the customer manages the OS upward. In PaaS the provider also manages the OS.","Objective 2.5",["In SaaS the provider manages the entire application, so the customer would not patch the OS.","In PaaS the provider manages the OS and runtime, leaving the customer only apps and data.","FaaS is serverless functions where the customer never manages an OS at all.",null],null],["sv35",0,2,"In a PowerShell script, which comparison operator tests whether $disk.FreeGB is less than 10?",["$disk.FreeGB -less 10","$disk.FreeGB < 10","$disk.FreeGB -lt 10","$disk.FreeGB -le 1"],2,"PowerShell uses -lt for less than; the < symbol is a redirection operator in PowerShell, not a comparison.","Objective 2.6",["-less is not a valid PowerShell comparison operator.","In PowerShell the < symbol is a redirection operator, not a less-than comparison.",null,"-le is less-than-or-equal, and this option compares to 1 rather than to 10."],null],["sv36",0,2,"An admin needs a Bash script to run every night at 2:00 AM on a Linux server. What should be used?",["A cron job","A systemd mount unit","An .bashrc entry","The at command once"],0,"cron runs scheduled jobs repeatedly (for example `0 2 * * *`). The at command runs a job only once.","Objective 2.6",[null,"A systemd mount unit mounts filesystems and is not a job scheduler.","An .bashrc entry runs at interactive shell login, not on a nightly schedule.","The at command schedules a job to run only once, not every night."],null],["sv37",0,2,"A script must create 200 user accounts listed in a CSV file. Which scripting construct is most useful?",["A comparator","A loop","A single variable","An exit code"],1,"A loop (such as foreach) repeats the account creation for each row. Comparators only test values.","Objective 2.6",["A comparator only tests values and cannot repeat the account-creation task for each row.",null,"A single variable holds one value and cannot iterate over 200 CSV rows.","An exit code reports whether the script succeeded, not a way to repeat an action."],null],["sv38",0,2,"What is the main risk of storing an administrator password in plain text inside a script?",["The script will run slower","The script cannot be scheduled","Anyone who reads the script gains the credential","It prevents the script's variables from being passed to functions"],2,"Plain-text credentials can be read by anyone with access to the file or its backups. Use a secrets vault, managed service accounts or encrypted credentials instead.","Objective 2.6",["Storing a password in plain text does not affect how fast the script runs.","A plain-text credential does not stop the script from being scheduled.",null,"Passing a variable to a function still works; plain-text storage does not prevent that."],null],["sv39",0,2,"A database product is licensed per core. A company replaces a 16-core server with one that has 64 cores. What happens to licensing cost?",["It increases because more cores must be licensed","It stays the same since it is one server","It decreases because newer CPUs complete the same work in less time","It changes only if users increase"],0,"Per-core licensing scales with core count, so four times the cores means more licenses. Per-server or per-user models would not change.","Objective 2.8",[null,"Per-core licensing is not per-server, so quadrupling the cores raises the license count.","Faster completion does not reduce the number of cores that must be licensed.","Per-core cost depends on cores, not on the number of users."],null],["sv40",0,2,"Each of 300 employees accesses a Windows file server from several devices. Which Client Access License model is usually more economical?",["Per-device CALs","Site license","Per-socket licenses","Per-user CALs"],3,"Per-user CALs cover a person on any number of devices, so they are cheaper when users have several devices. Per-device suits shared devices.","Objective 2.8",["Per-device CALs cost more when each user connects from several devices.","A site license is broad and usually far more expensive than needed for 300 users.","Per-socket licensing is based on server hardware and is unrelated to user counts.",null],null],["sv41",0,2,"An auditor asks when each server's hardware warranty expires. Which document should hold this?",["The asset inventory","The network diagram","The change log","The service's runbook"],0,"Asset records track serials, purchase dates, warranty and life-cycle status. Network diagrams show connections.","Objective 2.7",[null,"A network diagram shows how devices connect, not hardware warranty expiration dates.","A change log records modifications made to systems, not warranty information.","A runbook documents operating procedures, not asset warranty details."],null],["sv42",0,2,"A team documents normal CPU, memory and disk use for a new server during its first month. What is this record called?",["An SLA","A baseline","A service catalog","A root cause analysis"],1,"A performance baseline records normal behaviour so later anomalies stand out. An SLA defines service commitments.","Objective 2.7",["An SLA defines service commitments and targets, not the measured normal resource usage.",null,"A service catalog lists the services offered to users, not performance measurements.","A root cause analysis explains an incident afterward, not routine baseline behavior."],null],["sv43",0,2,"Before changing a production server's RAID configuration, what should normally be approved?",["A decommissioning ticket for the old array configuration","A new SLA","A license renewal","A change request through change management"],3,"Change management reviews risk, schedules a window and documents a backout plan before production changes. An SLA is unrelated to one change.","Objective 2.7",["A decommissioning ticket is for retiring assets, not for modifying a running array's config.","A new SLA sets service commitments and is not the approval needed for one config change.","A license renewal covers software entitlements and is unrelated to a RAID change.",null],null],["sv44",0,3,"A backup job runs a full on Sunday and incrementals Monday to Friday. The server fails Thursday afternoon. Which backups are needed to restore?",["Sunday's full and Wednesday's incremental, which includes all earlier changes","Only Thursday's incremental","Sunday's full and every incremental from Monday to Thursday","Sunday's full only"],2,"Each incremental holds only changes since the previous backup, so all of them are needed after the full. With differentials, only the latest would be needed.","Objective 3.7",["Sunday plus only Wednesday's incremental skips Thursday's changes and the other incrementals.","Thursday's incremental alone lacks the base full and all the intervening changes.",null,"The full alone would lose every change made during the week."],null],["sv45",0,3,"Restores must be as fast as possible using only two backup sets, and nightly backup time can grow during the week. Which scheme fits?",["Weekly full with daily differentials","Weekly full with daily incrementals","Monthly full only","Daily snapshots kept on the same volume"],0,"A differential holds all changes since the last full, so a restore needs just the full and the latest differential. Incrementals need every set in the chain.","Objective 3.7",[null,"Incrementals require restoring the full plus every set in the chain, more than two backup sets.","A monthly full alone leaves a huge data-loss window and no fast recent restore.","Daily snapshots on the same volume are not independent and are lost if that volume fails."],null],["sv46",0,3,"Which practice best follows the 3-2-1 backup rule?",["Three backups a day, two of them full, one of them tested","Three copies, on two media types, with one copy off-site","Three servers, two sites, one cloud","Three snapshots on two volumes of one array"],1,"3-2-1 means three copies of data on two kinds of media with one copy stored off-site. Snapshots on one array fail together.","Objective 3.7",["This describes backup frequency and testing, not the copies/media/off-site structure of 3-2-1.",null,"Counting servers and sites conflates infrastructure with the required data copies and media types.","Snapshots on one array all fail together and meet neither the two-media nor the off-site rule."],null],["sv47",0,3,"A company's backups run every night, but nobody has ever restored from them. What is the biggest risk?",["Backups take too long","Backup media will be full","The backups may be unusable when needed","Backup software licensing costs will rise every year"],2,"Untested backups may be corrupt, incomplete or missing data, which is only discovered during a disaster. Regular test restores prove recoverability.","Objective 3.7",["Long backup times are a performance concern, not the core danger of never testing restores.","Full media is a capacity issue and does not address whether the data can actually be recovered.",null,"Rising licensing costs are a budget matter, unrelated to whether the backups can restore."],null],["sv48",0,3,"A business can lose no more than 15 minutes of order data after an outage. Which metric does this define?",["RTO","MTTR","MTBF","RPO"],3,"RPO is the maximum tolerable data loss measured in time. RTO is how quickly service must be restored.","Objective 3.8",["RTO measures how quickly service must be restored, not how much data can be lost.","MTTR is the mean time to repair a failure, a reliability metric, not a data-loss target.","MTBF is the mean time between failures and does not describe tolerable data loss.",null],null],["sv49",0,3,"The payroll system must be running again within four hours of a disaster. Which metric does this define?",["RPO","MTBF","RTO","SLA credit"],2,"RTO is the maximum time allowed to restore a service. RPO measures acceptable data loss.","Objective 3.8",["RPO is the acceptable amount of lost data in time, not how fast service must return.","MTBF describes expected time between failures, not a recovery deadline.",null,"An SLA credit is compensation for a missed service level, not a recovery-time metric."],null],["sv50",0,3,"A company needs a DR site that can take over within minutes with current data. Cost is a secondary concern. Which site type fits?",["Cold site","Warm site","Reciprocal agreement","Hot site"],3,"A hot site has running equipment and up-to-date data for near-immediate failover. A warm site needs data restored first.","Objective 3.8",["A cold site has only space and power, taking days to weeks to bring online.","A warm site has hardware but needs data restored first, so it cannot fail over in minutes.","A reciprocal agreement relies on another organization's site with uncertain readiness.",null],null],["sv51",0,3,"Data is replicated to a DR site 800 km away. Writes must not wait for the remote site to confirm. Which replication type fits?",["Synchronous","Asynchronous","Snapshot-only","Mirrored RAID 1"],1,"Asynchronous replication acknowledges writes locally and sends them later, avoiding long-distance latency at the cost of some possible data loss. Synchronous waits for both sites.","Objective 3.8",["Synchronous replication waits for the remote site to confirm each write, adding heavy latency over 800 km.",null,"Snapshot-only replication captures periodic points in time rather than continuously sending writes.","Mirrored RAID 1 duplicates disks locally and does not replicate to a distant DR site."],null],["sv52",0,3,"Managers meet to talk through each step of the DR plan for a simulated flood without touching any systems. What kind of test is this?",["Tabletop exercise","Live failover test","Parallel test","Full interruption test"],0,"A tabletop exercise walks through the plan in discussion only. Live failover actually moves production to the DR site.","Objective 3.8",[null,"A live failover test actually shifts production to the DR site, which this exercise avoids.","A parallel test runs DR systems alongside production using real infrastructure, not just discussion.","A full interruption test shuts down production to prove DR, the most disruptive kind."],null],["sv53",0,3,"Old SSDs from retired servers must be made unrecoverable before disposal. Which method is reliable?",["Degaussing","Quick format","Deleting the partitions","Physical shredding"],3,"Shredding destroys the flash chips. Degaussing does not affect SSDs, and formatting or deleting partitions leaves data recoverable.","Objective 3.6",["Degaussing erases magnetic media and has no effect on an SSD's flash memory.","A quick format only clears the file table and leaves the underlying data recoverable.","Deleting partitions removes the partition table but leaves the actual data intact.",null],null],["sv54",0,3,"After a vendor destroys a batch of old hard drives, what should the company keep for its records?",["A certificate of destruction","The drives' warranty cards","A new baseline","The original purchase order and shipping receipts"],0,"A certificate of destruction documents which assets were destroyed, when and how, supporting audits and compliance.","Objective 3.6",[null,"Warranty cards concern support entitlements, not proof that the drives were destroyed.","A new baseline records performance behavior and is unrelated to media disposal.","Purchase orders and shipping receipts document acquisition, not secure destruction."],null],["sv55",0,3,"A new web server has FTP, Telnet and a print spooler running but only needs HTTPS. What is the first hardening step?",["Install a second NIC","Disable or remove unneeded services","Increase the RAID level","Enable jumbo frames on the server's network interface"],1,"Removing services that are not needed closes ports and reduces attack surface. Telnet also sends credentials in clear text.","Objective 3.5",["Adding a second NIC provides network redundancy, not a reduction in attack surface.",null,"Raising the RAID level improves storage resilience but does not harden the OS.","Enabling jumbo frames tunes network throughput and is not a security hardening step."],null],["sv56",0,3,"Admins connect to Linux servers with passwords over SSH, and logs show constant brute-force attempts. Which change most improves security?",["Move SSH to Telnet","Allow direct root login so fixes can be made faster","Use key-based authentication and disable password login","Turn off the host firewall"],2,"Key-based SSH defeats password guessing entirely. Telnet is unencrypted, and allowing root login increases risk.","Objective 3.5",["Telnet is unencrypted and far less secure than SSH, so moving to it worsens security.","Allowing direct root login increases the risk that a compromise gains full control.",null,"Turning off the host firewall exposes more services and weakens security."],null],["sv57",0,3,"A help desk group needs to reset passwords but must not manage servers. Which principle guides this?",["Separation of duties","Defense in depth","Least privilege","Implicit trust"],2,"Least privilege grants only the rights needed for a job. Separation of duties splits critical tasks among people, which is related but different.","Objective 3.3",["Separation of duties splits critical tasks among people; it is related but not about minimal rights.","Defense in depth layers multiple controls and is not specifically about limiting account rights.",null,"Implicit trust is the opposite of secure design and grants access without verification."],null],["sv58",0,3,"Accounts are assigned to groups like 'DB-Admins' and permissions are granted to the groups. What model is this?",["Discretionary access control","Role-based access control","Mandatory access control","Rule-based access control"],1,"RBAC grants rights to roles or groups, and users inherit them through membership. MAC uses labels and clearances.","Objective 3.3",["Discretionary access control lets object owners set permissions individually, not via role groups.",null,"Mandatory access control uses security labels and clearances enforced by the system.","Rule-based access control applies conditions like time or ACLs, not role membership."],null],["sv59",0,3,"Which physical control prevents one person from following another through a secured data center door?",["An access control vestibule (mantrap)","Badge reader alone","CCTV camera","Locked rack doors"],0,"A vestibule lets one person through at a time between two doors, stopping tailgating. A camera only records it.","Objective 3.2",[null,"A badge reader alone does not stop a second person from slipping through behind an authorized one.","A CCTV camera records tailgating after the fact but does not physically prevent it.","Locked rack doors protect the equipment racks, not the data center entrance."],null],["sv60",0,3,"A laptop holding a copy of a server database was stolen. What control would most likely have protected the data?",["NIC teaming","A cable lock on the server","A longer DHCP lease","Full-disk encryption"],3,"Full-disk encryption keeps data at rest unreadable without the key. The stolen device, not the server, held the data.","Objective 3.1",["NIC teaming provides network redundancy and does nothing to protect stored data.","A cable lock secures the server, but here it was the laptop that was stolen.","A longer DHCP lease only affects addressing and offers no data protection.",null],null],["sv61",0,3,"An admin wants to stop anyone at the console from changing boot order to start a live USB OS. What should be set?",["A longer screen saver timeout","A BIOS/UEFI administrator password","A DNS PTR record","Port security on the switch port the server uses"],1,"A firmware password blocks unauthorised changes to boot settings. Screen savers do not protect firmware.","Objective 3.1",["A screen saver timeout is an OS-level lock and does not stop firmware or boot-order changes.",null,"A DNS PTR record maps an IP to a name and is unrelated to firmware security.","Switch port security controls MAC addresses on the network, not the server's boot settings."],null],["sv62",0,3,"Which tool can detect and block sensitive files, such as customer records, from being copied to USB drives or emailed out?",["MPIO","IDS","NTP","DLP"],3,"Data loss prevention inspects data in use and in motion and blocks transfers that match policy. An IDS detects network attacks.","Objective 3.4",["MPIO manages redundant storage paths and has nothing to do with blocking data exfiltration.","An IDS detects network intrusions but does not enforce policy on files copied to USB or email.","NTP synchronizes clocks and provides no data-loss protection.",null],null],["sv63",0,3,"Ransomware encrypted a file server and also the backups stored on a network share it could reach. Which change best protects future backups?",["Keep an offline or immutable copy of backups","Back up more often to the same share","Switch to differential backups","Use a larger RAID 6 array with more parity drives"],0,"Offline, air-gapped or immutable backups cannot be altered by ransomware on the network. Backing up more often to the same share leaves them exposed.","Objective 3.4",[null,"Backing up more often to the same reachable share still leaves those copies open to encryption.","Choosing differential backups changes the backup type but not the ransomware's ability to reach them.","A larger RAID 6 array protects against disk failure, not against ransomware encrypting the files."],null],["sv64",0,3,"A backup rotation keeps daily, weekly and monthly sets, reusing the daily media each week. What scheme is this?",["Tower of Hanoi","3-2-1","Grandfather-father-son","First in, first out"],2,"GFS keeps sons (daily), fathers (weekly) and grandfathers (monthly) for longer retention. 3-2-1 is a copy-placement rule, not a rotation.","Objective 3.7",["Tower of Hanoi is a different rotation based on recursive reuse intervals, not daily/weekly/monthly sets.","3-2-1 is a copy-placement rule about media and off-site copies, not a retention rotation.",null,"FIFO simply overwrites the oldest media and lacks the tiered daily/weekly/monthly retention."],null],["sv65",0,3,"Company policy says financial records must be kept for seven years and then deleted. What is this policy called?",["Data classification","Data masking","Data sovereignty","Data retention"],3,"A retention policy states how long data is kept and when it is disposed of. Data sovereignty concerns which country's laws apply to where data is stored.","Objective 3.1",["Data classification labels how sensitive data is, not how long it must be kept.","Data masking obscures values to protect them and is unrelated to retention periods.","Data sovereignty concerns which country's laws govern where data is stored.",null],null],["sv66",0,4,"A technician has identified the problem with a server. What is the next step in the CompTIA troubleshooting methodology?",["Establish a theory of probable cause","Implement the solution","Document the findings","Verify full system functionality"],0,"After identifying the problem, the technician forms a theory of probable cause and then tests it. Implementing comes later.","Objective 4.1",[null,"Implementing the solution comes only after a plan of action, much later in the process.","Documenting findings is the final step, not what follows problem identification.","Verifying functionality happens near the end, after the solution is implemented."],null],["sv67",0,4,"Before making changes to fix a failing server, the technician should do what as part of identifying the problem?",["Replace the motherboard","Perform a backup if possible","Document lessons learned","Escalate immediately"],1,"The methodology says to back up where possible before changes so data is safe. Documenting comes last.","Objective 4.1",["Replacing the motherboard is a fix action, not part of identifying the problem.",null,"Documenting lessons learned is the last step of the methodology, not an early one.","Escalating immediately skips the investigation that identification requires."],null],["sv68",0,4,"A technician fixed a failed service and confirmed users can work. What should happen last?",["Question the users again","Establish a new theory","Document findings, actions and outcomes","Reboot the server"],2,"Documentation is the final step, so the fix is recorded for future incidents. Verifying functionality comes just before it.","Objective 4.1",["Questioning users is part of identifying the problem, not the closing step.","Establishing a theory happens early, well before the fix is verified.",null,"Rebooting is a possible action, not the final documentation step."],null],["sv69",0,4,"A server beeps at power-on and does not reach the OS. Which is the most likely cause to check first?",["A DNS misconfiguration","An expired certificate","A POST hardware failure such as memory","A full data volume"],2,"Beep codes are POST messages about hardware, commonly memory, CPU or video. DNS and certificates matter only after the OS loads.","Objective 4.2",["A DNS misconfiguration only matters once the OS has loaded, not at power-on beeps.","An expired certificate is an OS- or application-level issue, not a POST hardware fault.",null,"A full data volume is an OS-level problem and would not produce power-on beep codes."],null],["sv70",0,4,"A server shuts down on its own every afternoon, and the BMC log shows high inlet temperature events. What is the most likely cause?",["Cooling problems in the room or rack","A failing NIC","An OS memory leak","A bad DNS record"],0,"High inlet temperature means the air coming in is too warm, pointing to HVAC, blocked airflow or hot air recirculation. A NIC failure would not trigger thermal shutdown.","Objective 4.2",[null,"A failing NIC affects networking and would not trigger a thermal shutdown.","A memory leak causes gradual slowdown or crashes, not high inlet-temperature events.","A bad DNS record affects name resolution, not the server's temperature."],null],["sv71",0,4,"The OS reports corrected memory errors on one DIMM several times a day. What should the admin do?",["Ignore them since ECC corrected them","Schedule replacement of that DIMM","Increase swap space","Disable ECC in firmware"],1,"Rising correctable errors often lead to uncorrectable ones, so the DIMM should be replaced in a maintenance window. Disabling ECC removes protection.","Objective 4.2",["Ignoring rising correctable errors is risky because they often precede uncorrectable failures.",null,"Increasing swap space is disk paging and does nothing to fix a degrading DIMM.","Disabling ECC removes the very protection that is catching these errors."],null],["sv72",0,4,"One power supply in a redundant pair shows an amber LED, but the server is still running. What is the right action?",["Shut the server down immediately","Update the OS","Move all load to a Type 2 hypervisor","Replace the failed hot-swap PSU and check its power feed"],3,"Redundant PSUs keep the server running, but the failed unit (or its feed) must be fixed to restore redundancy. An immediate shutdown is unnecessary.","Objective 4.2",["Shutting down immediately is unnecessary because the redundant supply keeps the server running.","Updating the OS is irrelevant to a failed power supply.","Moving load to a Type 2 hypervisor makes no sense as a response to a PSU fault.",null],null],["sv73",0,4,"A RAID 5 array shows as degraded after one drive failed. What is the main risk until it is rebuilt?",["Slower DNS lookups","The OS license will expire","The array will automatically become RAID 0","A second drive failure will lose the array"],3,"RAID 5 tolerates only one failure; another failure before the rebuild finishes destroys the array. Replace the drive promptly and check backups.","Objective 4.3",["Slower DNS lookups are unrelated to the state of a degraded RAID array.","An OS license does not expire because an array is degraded.","A degraded RAID 5 array does not automatically convert itself to RAID 0.",null],null],["sv74",0,4,"After a power outage, a server shows 'boot device not found'. The drives spin up normally. What should the admin check first?",["The boot order and RAID controller configuration in firmware","The DNS server","The license key","The NIC teaming mode"],0,"A reset firmware or controller setting can change boot order or hide the array. DNS and licensing do not affect whether the disk is found at boot.","Objective 4.3",[null,"DNS plays no part in whether the firmware can locate a boot device.","A license key affects OS activation, not the hardware's ability to find the disk at boot.","NIC teaming is a network setting and has nothing to do with locating the boot drive."],null],["sv75",0,4,"SMART data for a drive shows a rising count of reallocated sectors. What does this indicate?",["The drive is healthy and optimizing","The drive is likely to fail and should be replaced","The file system needs defragmenting","The RAID controller cache is full"],1,"Reallocated sectors mean the drive is remapping bad areas; a growing count predicts failure. Defragmenting will not help.","Objective 4.3",["Reallocated sectors signal degradation, not a healthy drive optimizing itself.",null,"Defragmenting rearranges files and cannot repair physically bad sectors.","A full controller cache is unrelated to a drive's SMART reallocated-sector count."],null],["sv76",0,4,"A Linux server's application crashes with 'No space left on device', yet `df -h` shows 40% free. What should the admin check next?",["The DHCP lease","The default gateway","Inode usage with df -i","The SSH key"],2,"A volume can run out of inodes when it holds many small files, even with free blocks. `df -i` shows inode use.","Objective 4.3",["A DHCP lease concerns IP addressing and has nothing to do with a disk-space error.","The default gateway is a routing setting, unrelated to running out of space.",null,"An SSH key is for authentication and does not affect storage capacity."],null],["sv77",0,4,"After an unclean shutdown, a Linux data volume will not mount and reports file system errors. Which tool repairs it?",["chmod on the mount point","fsck on the unmounted volume","ping to the storage array","top"],1,"fsck checks and repairs Linux file systems and must be run while the volume is unmounted. chmod changes permissions only.","Objective 4.3",["chmod changes permissions on the mount point and does not repair a corrupted file system.",null,"ping only tests network reachability to the storage array, not file system integrity.","top displays running processes and cannot repair a file system."],null],["sv78",0,4,"Disk write latency on a RAID controller jumped after the controller's battery failed. What most likely happened?",["The array changed RAID level","The NIC dropped to 100 Mbps","The drives were set to read-only","The controller switched from write-back to write-through cache"],3,"Without a healthy battery or flash backup, controllers disable write-back caching to protect data, so writes go straight to disk and slow down.","Objective 4.3",["A failed cache battery does not change the array's RAID level.","The NIC speed is a network matter and is unrelated to disk write latency.","A dead cache battery does not set the drives to read-only.",null],null],["sv79",0,4,"A Windows server's available memory drops steadily over two weeks until it slows, and a restart fixes it for a while. What is the most likely cause?",["A memory leak in a process","A failed power supply","A duplex mismatch","An incorrect subnet mask"],0,"Gradual memory loss that a reboot clears is typical of a memory leak. Trending process memory in Performance Monitor finds the culprit.","Objective 4.4",[null,"A failed power supply causes an outage, not a slow, steady decline in available memory.","A duplex mismatch degrades network performance, not memory availability.","An incorrect subnet mask affects connectivity, not the server's memory usage."],null],["sv80",0,4,"A Windows service fails to start after the service account's password was changed. What is the most likely fix?",["Reinstall the OS","Change the service to manual","Update the password stored in the service's Log On settings","Add more RAM"],2,"Services running under a domain account keep a copy of the password, which must be updated after a change. Reinstalling is excessive.","Objective 4.4",["Reinstalling the OS is far more work than needed to fix a changed service-account password.","Setting the service to manual changes its startup type but does not fix the wrong stored password.",null,"Adding RAM is unrelated to a service failing due to a credential change."],null],["sv81",0,4,"After a monthly patch, a critical application on a server stops working. Other servers without the patch are fine. What should the admin do first?",["Change the server's IP address","Disable the host firewall","Replace the server","Roll back the patch per the change plan and test"],3,"The patch is the likely cause, so rolling it back restores service while the issue is researched. Disabling the firewall adds risk and does not address the change.","Objective 4.4",["Changing the server's IP address is unrelated to a failure caused by a patch.","Disabling the host firewall adds risk and does not address the offending patch.","Replacing the server is drastic when the recent patch is the obvious suspect.",null],null],["sv82",0,4,"A Linux service fails to start. Which command shows its recent log messages?",["journalctl -u <service>","ip route","df -h","lsblk"],0,"journalctl with -u filters the systemd journal for that unit, showing why it failed. lsblk lists block devices.","Objective 4.4",[null,"ip route displays the routing table, not a service's log messages.","df -h shows disk space usage, not why a service failed.","lsblk lists block devices and reveals nothing about a service's logs."],null],["sv83",0,4,"A server can reach other hosts on its subnet but nothing on remote networks. What is the most likely cause?",["A wrong DNS server","A wrong or missing default gateway","A failed RAID array","An expired certificate"],1,"Traffic to other networks goes through the default gateway; if it is wrong, only local hosts work. DNS affects name resolution, not reachability by IP.","Objective 4.5",["A wrong DNS server breaks name resolution but not reachability to remote networks by IP.",null,"A failed RAID array is a storage issue and does not affect network reachability.","An expired certificate is an application-security problem, not a routing failure."],null],["sv84",0,4,"Users can reach a server by its IP address but not by its name. Which tool should the admin use first?",["arp -a","tracert","nslookup or dig","netstat -r"],2,"Name failure with working IP access points to DNS, which nslookup or dig tests directly. tracert tests the path, not naming.","Objective 4.5",["arp -a shows the local IP-to-MAC cache and does not test DNS name resolution.","tracert maps the network path by hops, not whether a name resolves.",null,"netstat -r prints the routing table, which is unrelated to name resolution."],null],["sv85",0,4,"A web app on a server is unreachable from clients, though ping works. The admin wants to test whether TCP port 443 is open from a client. Which command fits?",["arp -d","ipconfig /flushdns","Test-NetConnection server -Port 443","chkdsk /f"],2,"Test-NetConnection (or telnet/nc) attempts a TCP connection to a port, showing whether the service or firewall blocks it. flushdns only clears the name cache.","Objective 4.5",["arp -d clears the ARP cache and does not test whether a TCP port is open.","ipconfig /flushdns clears the DNS cache and does not probe a port.",null,"chkdsk /f checks a disk for errors and has nothing to do with testing a port."],null],["sv86",0,4,"A server's link is slow and the switch port shows many late collisions, while the server side shows none. What is the most likely cause?",["A duplex mismatch","DNS misconfiguration","A wrong VLAN","A bad gateway"],0,"Late collisions on one side mean one end is half duplex and the other full. Set both ends to match or to autonegotiate.","Objective 4.5",[null,"A DNS misconfiguration would break name resolution, not cause late collisions on a link.","A wrong VLAN would isolate traffic rather than produce late collisions.","A bad gateway is a routing problem and does not generate collisions."],null],["sv87",0,4,"A server with a static IP shows 'duplicate IP address' warnings after a new printer was added. What is the most likely cause?",["The server's clock is wrong","The server's NIC is failing","The DNS zone is corrupt","The printer got the server's address from the DHCP pool"],3,"If a static address sits inside the DHCP scope, a new device can be leased the same address. Exclude or reserve static addresses.","Objective 4.5",["A wrong clock is a time-sync issue and does not create a duplicate IP address.","A failing NIC would cause link problems, not a duplicate-address warning.","A corrupt DNS zone affects name resolution, not IP address conflicts.",null],null],["sv88",0,4,"After a server's TLS certificate expired, clients see security warnings. What should the admin do?",["Disable TLS on the server","Renew and install a new certificate, and set a renewal reminder","Change the server's IP address","Restart the DNS service"],1,"An expired certificate must be replaced; tracking or automating renewal prevents recurrence. Disabling TLS removes encryption.","Objective 4.6",["Disabling TLS removes encryption entirely and leaves clients unprotected.",null,"Changing the server's IP address has no bearing on an expired certificate.","Restarting the DNS service does not renew or replace an expired certificate."],null],["sv89",0,4,"A user can open a shared folder but cannot save files, even though the share permission is Full Control. What is the most likely cause?",["The DNS record is wrong","The NTFS permissions only allow Read","The server has no default gateway","The disk is in RAID 1"],1,"Share and NTFS permissions combine and the more restrictive applies, so Read on NTFS blocks writes. DNS problems would block opening the share at all.","Objective 4.6",["A wrong DNS record would prevent reaching the share at all, not just block saving files.",null,"A missing gateway would block access entirely, yet the user can already open the share.","RAID 1 is disk redundancy and is not a permission control that would block writes."],null],["sv90",0,4,"After installing new endpoint protection, a line-of-business app on a server stops launching. What is the most likely cause?",["The NIC team split","The RAID battery failed","The app's files were quarantined or blocked by the security software","The DHCP scope is exhausted"],2,"New antivirus or EDR can flag legitimate executables; check its quarantine and add a vetted exclusion. The other issues would not appear right after that install.","Objective 4.6",["A split NIC team is a network issue and would not stop an application from launching.","A failed RAID battery slows disk writes but would not block an app right after an AV install.",null,"An exhausted DHCP scope affects addressing, not an app blocked following an EDR install."],null]]);
